# platform.pico.mcu

The Pico platform's implementation of mm.mcu. It is project-owned platform code,
not the public wrapper for a vendored library: it therefore takes no lib. prefix
and declares no library. Its availability comes from the platform-provider
binding in the pico-arm and pico-riscv SDK definitions, which is also what makes
it unavailable in any lane those SDKs do not select.

One provider module serves both SDKs and every Raspberry Pi Pico board
declaration. Two SDKs binding the same provider is one module with one identity,
not two implementations that happen to agree.

The bridge reports the selected Pico board name, GPIO count, and any GPIO-backed
LED. This module turns those private values into mm.mcu's Board, Gpio, and Led
values, so portable applications neither see SDK macros nor hard-code board
properties. GPIO operations use Pico SDK GPIO, SPI instances zero and one use
Pico SDK hardware_spi, portable UART instance zero uses
the selected board's default hardware UART and pins, and timer operations use
Pico SDK's monotonic time. Pico W LEDs and wireless facilities remain outside
this interface because their LED is attached to the CYW43, not to an RP GPIO.

Beneath its pure C++ surface it calls a private, project-owned C ABI implemented
by the bridge's adapter, the only translation unit permitted to include Pico SDK
headers. That ABI is specific to this bridge; it is not the portable mm.mcu
contract, and nothing outside this directory declares or calls it.
